.SH NAME
mv  \- move or rename files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mv
\fIfile1 file2\fR
.PP
.B mv
\fIfile\fR ... \fIdirectory\fR
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mv
moves (changes the name of)
.I file1
to
.IR file2 .
.PP
If
.I file2
already exists, it is removed before
.I file1
is moved.
If
.I file2
has a mode which forbids writing,
.B mv
prints the mode (see
.BR chmod (2))
and reads the standard input to obtain a line;
if the line begins with
.B y,
the move takes place;  if not,
.B mv
exits.
.PP
In the second form,
one or more
.I files
are moved to the
.I directory
with their original
file-names.
.PP
.B Mv
refuses to move a file onto itself.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"cannot link /dir2/dir1 to /dir1"	Printed if not superuser.
.SH SEE ALSO
cat(1), cp(1), chmod(2).
.SH LIMITATIONS
If
.I file1
and
.I file2
lie on different file systems,
.B mv
must copy the file and delete the original.
In this case the owner name becomes that of the copying process
and any linking relationship with other files is lost.
